A||||10||88formula||Handling with engine swap||||5||
Z||000000||88formula||01-14-2000||09:06 PM||||For all you Fiero owners out there with a 3800 engine swap, how does you Fiero handle compared to your old setup? Does the back end feel heavier? How does it corner compared to the original engine?||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||01-15-2000||04:52 PM||||I guess no one knows about this one.||||reg||13
Z||000002||Phil||01-15-2000||05:39 PM||||Never drove mine without the 3800.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||01-15-2000||07:32 PM||||Phil, you've driven other Fieros though. Tells us how it handles compared to others you've driven. The weight difference isn't that much, so I suspect there isn't much change in handling. Nothing a rear sway bar wouldn't help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||01-15-2000||08:01 PM||||Formula, If you know what the weight difference is try adding that amount to your trunk.  It won't be exact (because of the location) but it may give you a pretty good idea of what to expect.  Unfortunately, there is not a way to simulate the extra power (except maybe driving in the next lower gear than normal).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||01-15-2000||08:05 PM||||How do I find out the weights of the engines fully dressed?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||01-15-2000||09:21 PM||||Actually I've only driven one other Fiero and that was my son's 88GT while I was fixing it up about 30 mi total.  But I've put 15K on my 3800 since Aug. My kitcar 85GT Lambo with the 3.2 has only been up and down the driveway. I don't drive in a very spirited manner anyway (old Fa*t ya know). I must be nuts though I'm working on getting 2 more 87 very base coupe power nuttin 4cly 5sp roughshape ($100) and 88 Formula $?||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||01-15-2000||10:04 PM||||My gut feeling is, if you moved the battery up front under the spare tire like what I'm doing to the Batmobile, that ought to make up for any weight gain on a 3800 engine swap project.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tom88gt||01-16-2000||12:11 PM||||here is a guess, ONLY a guess at engine wieghts.  I got them from a KEP catalog in 1996 for my 914 engine swap.<P>Buick V-6  225cid 64-70, 3.8 75-86, 4.1 80+,  367-410 lbs <P>Buick 3.0 82+, 3.3 89+,  377 lbs<P>Buick 198 cid 62-63, 345 lbs<P>Chevy 2.8 79-89, 3.1 90+, 350 lbs<P>These weights were supposedly all verified by KEP at their shop without the flywheel and options.<P>My guess is that you will gain about 25 lbs.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||7
Z||000009||Monkeyman||01-16-2000||01:18 PM||||According to V8Archie, you only gain about 150# with a fully dressed V8, so I don't think that the 3800 could add very much.  I'd go with Tom88gts weight.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||01-16-2000||11:14 PM||||My personl experience with driving a 3.8sc (my dads car) vs my 2.8 gave me the impression that the additional torque affects the handling more than the weight.  Of course when his swap was fnished it did have a rear sway bar, KYB struts and lowering springs, but if the back end would start to slide, mashing the throttle got the momentum going in the right direction quickly.  I'm no physicist, but I don't think the extra weight is a bad thing.||||reg||1
